---
title: Chimichurri
description: Lesson 7.2. Mincing herbs and garlic, and how the cut sets the strength of garlic.
servings: 6
time: 20 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, mastering-the-knife]
lesson: "7.2"
technique: mincing
status: draft
---

> Technique: mincing. Mincing takes something already chopped and
> reduces it to very small, even pieces by rocking the knife through
> it again and again. In a raw sauce like this one, how finely you cut
> is the recipe.

Peel @garlic{2%cloves}. Slice them thinly, then chop across the slices. Sprinkle over @coarse salt{1/2%tsp}, lay the side of the blade nearly flat on the pile and drag it across, pressing, a few times, until the garlic is a paste. Scrape it into a #bowl{} and leave it while you do the herbs.

> Science: garlic's flavour comes from allicin, which does not exist in
> a whole clove. It forms when the cell walls are broken, and the more
> they are broken the more forms: none in a whole clove, a little in
> slices, more in a mince, the most in a paste. It keeps forming after
> the cutting stops, so garlic left for ten minutes is stronger and
> more complex than garlic used at once. Look up: allicin.
-- source: Carolyn Grillo, "The Strength of Garlic's Flavor Depends on How You Cut It", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-05-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3693-the-strength-of-garlic-s-flavor-depends-on-how-you-cut-it

> Why the salt: dragging the blade over coarsely salted garlic breaks
> down as many cells as possible, which is what makes a paste the
> strongest form.
-- source: Carolyn Grillo, "The Strength of Garlic's Flavor Depends on How You Cut It", America's Test Kitchen, 2022-05-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/3693-the-strength-of-garlic-s-flavor-depends-on-how-you-cut-it

Pick the leaves from @flat-leaf parsley{30%g} and gather them into a tight pile. Slice across the pile. Then hold the handle in one hand, rest the fingers of the other flat on the spine near the tip, keep the tip on the board, and rock the blade up and down while you sweep it across the pile. Gather and repeat until the parsley is fine.

> Why this grip here: the test kitchen recommends holding the knife by
> the handle for only two jobs, honing it and mincing herbs. For
> everything else, pinch the blade.
-- source: Liz Bomze, "A Guide to Using Your Chef's Knife", America's Test Kitchen, 2018-12-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/1362-a-guide-to-using-your-chef-s-knife

Add the parsley to the bowl with @dried oregano{1%tsp}, @chilli flakes{1/4%tsp}, @red wine vinegar{2%tbsp} and @olive oil{6%tbsp}. Stir, taste, and adjust the salt and vinegar.

> Next time, without the recipe: decide how strong you want the
> garlic, then cut it to match: sliced for mild, minced for strong,
> pasted for strongest. Cut it first and let it sit.
